C = [
("h1", "14. “All Things Are Lawful”"),
("p", "Claim: Paul writes “All things are lawful” twice, at 1 Corinthians 6:12 and 10:23, and each time he follows it with a limit, and the limits are different. Two other “all things” sentences in his letters have the same form. In every case the sentence states a liberty and is followed by “but.” The liberty is not denied, and it is not left unlimited."),
("sub", "14.1 The two places"),
("p", "“All things are lawful unto me, but all things are not expedient: all things are lawful for me, but I will not be brought under the power of any” (1 Corinthians 6:12). “All things are lawful for me, but all things are not expedient: all things are lawful for me, but all things edify not” (1 Corinthians 10:23). The sentences begin alike. Each says that all things are lawful and that all things are not expedient. They differ in the last clause. In chapter 6 the limit is mastery: “I will not be brought under the power of any.” In chapter 10 the limit is edification: “all things edify not.” The first is a limit on what a thing does to the one who uses it. The second is a limit on what it does to others."),
("sub", "14.2 The first place: the body is for the Lord"),
("p", "In chapter 6 the sentence stands among two others, “Meats for the belly, and the belly for meats: but God shall destroy both it and them” (1 Corinthians 6:13), and the answer, “Now the body is not for fornication, but for the Lord; and the Lord for the body” (1 Corinthians 6:13). The liberty is stated in the matter of food, and Paul turns at once to fornication, which he commands against: “Flee fornication” (1 Corinthians 6:18). The limit he gives is the body’s belonging: “What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own? For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God’s” (1 Corinthians 6:19, 20). The liberty is not a liberty to treat the body as one’s own."),
("p", "The preceding verses give the other side of the limit. Of the unrighteous who “shall not inherit the kingdom of God,” Paul writes, “And such were some of you: but ye are washed, but ye are sanctified, but ye are justified in the name of the Lord Jesus, and by the Spirit of our God” (1 Corinthians 6:9–11). The volume reads the passage as written and does not draw from it a rule about the believer’s standing. It notes only that the liberty of 6:12 is stated after a list in which there is no liberty."),
("sub", "14.3 The second place: edification"),
("p", "In chapter 10 the sentence stands before the course for the market and the feast, and its limit, “all things edify not,” is the limit of the neighbour’s good. “Let no man seek his own, but every man another’s wealth” (1 Corinthians 10:24). The sentence is not repeated as a protest against the strong. It is an item in the list of considerations the believer weighs. The volume reads the two limits together: one says what the thing does to the user, the other what it does to the neighbour. Neither is the other."),
("sub", "14.4 Was it a Corinthian saying?"),
("p", "Some have held that “All things are lawful” was a saying of the Corinthians which Paul quotes in order to limit it. Others hold that it was Paul’s own teaching, which some at Corinth had pushed too far. The text does not say. Paul writes “unto me” and “for me,” and he does not mark the sentence as another’s. The volume does not decide the question. The reading of the passages does not depend on it, since in either case Paul states the sentence and limits it, and the limits are what the volume reads."),
("sub", "14.5 The pattern of “all things”"),
("p", "Two other sentences in Paul’s letters have the same form. “All things indeed are pure; but it is evil for that man who eateth with offence” (Romans 14:20). “Unto the pure all things are pure: but unto them that are defiled and unbelieving is nothing pure; but even their mind and conscience is defiled” (Titus 1:15). In each the first clause grants the liberty and the second limits it. In Romans the limit is the offence to a brother. In Titus the limit is the condition of the person: purity is not given to the defiled. The volume does not say that Titus 1:15 answers the question of Romans 14. It notes the form. Paul does not leave “all things” standing alone."),
("ctbl", "The “all things” sentences and their limits.", [
    ("Passage", "The liberty stated", "The limit that follows"),
    ("1 Corinthians 6:12", "All things are lawful unto me", "Not brought under the power of any; the body is for the Lord"),
    ("1 Corinthians 10:23", "All things are lawful for me", "Not expedient; they edify not"),
    ("Romans 14:20", "All things indeed are pure", "Evil for that man who eateth with offence"),
    ("Titus 1:15", "Unto the pure all things are pure", "Nothing is pure to the defiled and unbelieving"),
    ("Galatians 5:13", "Ye have been called unto liberty", "Use not liberty for an occasion to the flesh; serve one another by love"),
  ], [0.24, 0.34, 0.42]),
("sub", "14.6 What the volume takes from the sentence"),
("bul", [
    "“All things are lawful” is stated twice, with two different limits: not mastered, and edification.",
    "Whether the Corinthians said it first is not stated, and the reading does not depend on it.",
    "In each “all things” sentence Paul grants the liberty and then limits it.",
    "The liberty is real and is never unlimited.",
  ]),
]
